    "answer": {
      "short": "$n=40$.\n\nExpanding the sum gives a quadratic whose minimum occurs at $x=\\frac n2$.",
      "worked": "$$\\begin{align*}y&=x^2+(x^2-2x+1)+\\cdots+(x^2-2nx+n^2)\\\\y&=(n+1)x^2-2(1+2+\\cdots+n)x+(1^2+2^2+\\cdots+n^2)\\\\y&=(n+1)x^2-n(n+1)x+\\frac{n(n+1)(2n+1)}6.\\end{align*}$$\nSince $n+1>0$, the parabola opens upwards. Its minimum occurs at\n$$\\begin{align*}x&=-\\frac{-n(n+1)}{2(n+1)}\\\\x&=\\frac n2.\\end{align*}$$\nThe given minimum is at $x=20$, so\n$$\\begin{align*}\\frac n2&=20\\\\n&=40.\\end{align*}$$",
      "solutionDiagrams": []
    },
